<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Continue Reading.

  4x1 by default. One row is the honest size: the answer is a surah and a
  page, and a card twice as tall as its own content is a void with a border
  round it. Resizable up for anyone who wants the progress bar and the
  khatmah portion with it, and down to 2x2 where the side column drops.

  Was 4x2, and every attempt to make that height look deliberate — centring
  the block, enlarging the type, pinning the header to one edge — only moved
  the emptiness around. Sizing the widget to what it says is the fix. The two columns need the width — a
  surah name and a portion counter side by side in half of it leaves neither
  readable — so below about four cells the provider drops the side column
  and the left one carries on answering the question the widget exists for:
  where was I. Both sizes are in the plan; that is what makes the small one
  honest rather than cramped.

  maxResizeHeight is 400dp, not 200. At 200 the card could not be dragged
  past about two rows, so "make it taller" was not an option a user had —
  and the sizes above two are the ones with room for the progress bar, the
  tail and type that is actually large. The provider sizes its content to
  what it is given (see Tier there), so the ceiling is only a ceiling.

  updatePeriodMillis is 0. A reading position moves when a page is turned
  and the app pushes then; what changes unattended is the day, which
  ACTION_PRAYER_TIME_ELAPSED already brings round.

  On `android:configure`, which this file used to refuse. The refusal was
  half right: a settings screen between the tap that places a widget and the
  first tap that uses it IS a worse first run, and that is what the comment
  here defended. But the screen sets background strength and accent colour,
  which every card honours — this one already draws through
  `PrayerWidgetProvider.resolvedColors` — so leaving the attribute off did
  not remove a prompt, it removed the only way in. Placed widgets had no
  settings at all while the three prayer-times entries did, which is what a
  user reported.

  `configuration_optional` is the flag that wanted using: the launcher
  places the widget immediately and the screen becomes an entry in the
  long-press menu instead of a gate in front of it. `reconfigurable` is what
  puts that entry there. Both are API 31; below that Android falls back to
  prompting on placement, the same as the prayer widgets have always done.
-->
<appwidget-provider xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:configure="com.prayer_times.PrayerWidgetConfigureActivity"
    android:widgetFeatures="reconfigurable|configuration_optional"
    android:description="@string/widget_description_reading"
    android:initialLayout="@layout/prayer_widget_reading"
    android:minWidth="250dp"
    android:minHeight="50dp"
    android:previewImage="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
    android:previewLayout="@layout/prayer_widget_reading_preview"
    android:resizeMode="horizontal|vertical"
    android:minResizeWidth="110dp"
    android:minResizeHeight="40dp"
    android:updatePeriodMillis="0"
    android:widgetCategory="home_screen"
    android:targetCellWidth="4"
    android:targetCellHeight="1"
    android:maxResizeWidth="500dp"
    android:maxResizeHeight="400dp" />
